Wait, I see it now! The check `if ( err || nbytes > matvar->nbytes )` is checking if `nbytes > matvar->nbytes`. But `nbytes = nelems * matvar->data_size` and `matvar->nbytes = nelems * matvar->data_size` (from the SafeMul above). So `nbytes == matvar->nbytes` always. The check is useless.

But the real issue is: `ReadCharData(mat, (char*)matvar->data, packed_type, (int)nelems)` reads `nelems` elements of type `packed_type`. If `packed_type` is MAT_T_UINT16, it reads `nelems * sizeof(uint16_t)` bytes from the file but only writes `nelems` bytes to the buffer. The buffer is `nelems` bytes. So no overflow.

But what if `packed_type` is MAT_T_DOUBLE? Then `ReadCharData` would go to the default case and just warn. No overflow.

